TTYSIZE(3)                     P32W Unix                      TTYSIZE(3)

NAME
       ttysize - the size of a terminal

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sgtty.h>

       int ttysize(int fd, int *cols, int *rows);

DESCRIPTION
       ttysize tells how many columns and lines the terminal has
       that fd is open on, for a program that fills a screen or
       sets things in columns. It takes each of the two from the
       first of these that has it:

       the system    what the kernel keeps for the terminal
                     (ioctl(2)'s TIOCGWINSZ): the video screen's
                     size, a window's under wm(1), what stty(1)
                     was told
       the variables COLUMNS and LINES
       80 and 24

       Either pointer may be 0 if that number is not wanted.

       The size may change while a program runs (a window is made
       bigger or smaller). A program that must know at once
       catches SIGWINCH (signal(2)) and asks again; one that draws
       its screen every so often can simply ask each time.

RETURN VALUE
       0 if fd is a terminal; -1 if it is not (the numbers are
       then the variables', or 80 and 24).

EXAMPLES
       int cols, rows;
       ttysize(1, &cols, &rows);

SEE ALSO
       ioctl(2), signal(2), stty(1), wm(1), getenv(3)
